.TH PASTE 1
.SH NAME
paste \- merge corresponding lines of several files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paste
.I file
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Paste
reads one line at a time from each
.I file
(the name
.L -
means standard input) and writes them to standard output joined by tabs,
one output line per set of input lines. When files run out of lines at
different times, the missing fields are left empty rather than stopping
the merge early; output continues until every file is exhausted.
.PP
The classic use is putting a file back together with a copy of itself
shifted by one line, to pair each item with the one that follows it.
.SH EXAMPLE
.TP
.EX
tr -cs A-Za-z '\en' <genesis >words
tail +2 words >next
paste words next | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr
.EE
.IP
The most common word pairs (bigrams) in
.BR genesis .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/paste.b
.SH BUGS
No
.B -d
(alternate delimiters) or
.B -s
(serial, one file's lines joined onto a single output line): neither was
needed to merge parallel files line by line, which is the one thing this
was built for.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tail (1),
.IR sort (1)
